WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.577
One correction is important before we start.

00:00:02.577 --> 00:00:07.957
PostgreSQL does not use DATEADD and DATEDIFF as native functions.

00:00:07.957 --> 00:00:15.077
Instead, PostgreSQL commonly uses date or timestamp subtraction, INTERVAL values, EXTRACT, DATE_TRUNC, AGE,

00:00:15.077 --> 00:00:17.737
TO_DATE, TO_TIMESTAMP, and TO_CHAR.

00:00:17.737 --> 00:00:23.822
The underlying analytical ideas are portable, but the SQL syntax depends on the database system.

00:00:23.822 --> 00:00:27.862
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:27.862 --> 00:00:34.987
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:34.987 --> 00:00:39.257
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:39.257 --> 00:00:43.862
In a real setting such as a CRM standardizing names, emails, and phone numbers,

00:00:43.862 --> 00:00:51.517
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:53.822
before discussing syntax.

00:00:53.822 --> 00:01:00.000
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
